By task (the same credits as the notebook):
- invsqrt (invsqrt/): our builder is a fork of Syed Asad Ali's public Apache-2.0 generator (the in-place root,
  quotient signs kept in freed wires, cyclic routing into the rotations). manwithacat's "VQCC Hedge Wizardry
  Notebook", tweak HW-27 (measure-first), applied by our own tool. Prior art that overlaps our exact post-pass and
  angle solve, credited to be safe: the measure-out rewrite (manwithacat, HW-26), identical-Toffoli-pair deletion in
  boristown's public qccscope package, the public AND merge of jeffemerson's notebook, and the linear-programming
  angle re-solve in the public notebooks of asteriospanoras and hayatohikawa.
- DQI (dqi/): our DQI builder started from the public source bundle of Atakan Aldemir and the co-credited authors
  (Syed Asad Ali, Lera / marculera, qwertykeypadapple, manwithacat). The upstream lineage credited inside that bundle
  is SomeJoint (the 892-qubit decoder), Ali Haydar Ozdag / yoyobar (carry; cache reuses), manwithacat (HW06 / HW07
  flag uncomputation templates), Craig Gidney (temporary-AND cleanup) and Busya PRIME / Ahmed Berat Ozer (product
  caching). James Day (jday96314), repository QuantumAI (Apache-2.0 since 2026-11-09), wrote the decoder design the
  public line descends from. Our projective finish and CRT closing follow Syed Asad Ali's public DQI decoder and were
  re-implemented in our code. Public mathematics: flip graphs (M. Kauers and J. Moosbauer, arXiv:2212.01175), flip
  graphs with plus transitions (Y. Arai, Y. Ichikawa and K. Hukushima, arXiv:2312.17094), and Toom-Cook evaluation and
  interpolation.
- Chemistry (chem/): the loaders' high-cache form at 68 ANDs and the "sector closure" were learned from Syed Asad
  Ali's public chemistry plans, read as data. Our fitting objective was first rebuilt from Lera / marculera's
  public Apache-2.0 pipeline (marculera/dfthc-compact-pipeline), and the public loader notebooks of Lera and maoxin
  were read for our loader engine's early ideas. The objective and the file format come from the hosts' Verifiable
  Circuit Compilation Starter Kit. Public mathematics: L. Lovasz's randomized algebraic test for linear matroid
  parity. The channel gauge (chem/tools/lead_gauge.py: the idea is public, the code is ours) follows these public
  notebooks: Kirill Tymchishin (exact gauge), Alex Chilton (LLM gauge), Agniv Banerjee / SomeJoint (exact gauge
  sweeps), Isabe11aM00re (per-rank column gauge), James Barlow / manwithacat (refit alternated with the column gauge).
- kickmix, the verifiers and the competition paper (Khattar et al., arXiv:2610.23832) are by Google Quantum AI. They
  are read from the competition data and are not redistributed here.
